(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2025-004"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2025-004","title":"Advance Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 DA","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2025","num":4,"category":"advance","year":"2025","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/007_Advance Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2025 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tNumber of divisors of 9000 that are multiple of 15.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"18"},{"key":"B","html":"27"},{"key":"C","html":"36"},{"key":"D","html":"24"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f96564649d61897e262a.png\" /></p>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tThe salaries of three friends Sita, Gita and Mita are initially in the ratio 5 : 6 : 7 respectively. In the first year, they get salary hikes of 20%, 25% and 20% respectively. In the second year, Sita and Mita get salary hikes of 40% and 25%, respectively, and the salary of Gita becomes equal to the mean salary of the three friends. The salary hike of Gita in the second year is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"25%"},{"key":"B","html":"28%"},{"key":"C","html":"26%"},{"key":"D","html":"30%"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/63a113d10b3cc976b4fb.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cf/cfe023e7e053af729ac3.png\" /></p>"},{"n":3,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\t<em>ABCDEF</em> is a regular hexagon of side 12 cm, the area of triangle <em>BDF</em> is ______ cm<sup>2</sup>.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[185.8,188.8],"answerText":"187 (185.8 - 188.8)","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t187 (185.8 - 188.8)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c5/c508f422359dea22b2af.png\" /></p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tIn a particular way of coding, HONESTY is coded as 5132468 and POVERTY is coded as 7192068.<br />\n\tIn the same way, HORSE can be written as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"50124"},{"key":"B","html":"51024"},{"key":"C","html":"51042"},{"key":"D","html":"52014"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\tH - 5<br />\n\tO - 1<br />\n\tR - 0<br />\n\tS - 4<br />\n\tE - 2</p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tExamine the following statements:<br />\n\t1. Rama scored more than Rani.<br />\n\t2. Rani scored less than Ratna.<br />\n\t3. Ratna scored more than Rama.<br />\n\t4. Padma scored more than Rama but less than Ratna.<br />\n\tWhich of the following options is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ratna scored highest"},{"key":"B","html":"Padma scored lowest"},{"key":"C","html":"Rama scored highest"},{"key":"D","html":"Rani scored lowest"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, d)<br />\n\tI : Rama &gt; Rani<br />\n\tII : Ratna &gt; Rani<br />\n\tIII : Ratna &gt; Rama<br />\n\tIV : Ratna &gt; Padma &gt; Rama<br />\n\tFrom the above Ratna &gt; Padma &gt; Rama &gt; Rani</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tSelect the figure from the options that can replace the question mark (?) and complete the pattern.</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9acf1a891af2ddd77498.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/551c20deff20d9d74f4b.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cd/cd4a9f1bdef275cd9f77.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e4/e47a84cc8277abadb8f7.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da4577daf7f5fed26fe0.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tSelect the option that is embedded in the given figure (rotation is NOT allowed).</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e8421214b10adf18d9b6.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/226bbc2ce6b231f4104c.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/63edeed00729e3ee2a2d.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe856a4be96c42abce9d.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/29/29834bc2312958eac775.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tHow many triangles are there in the given figure?</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0db4e882fd87255cab37.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8"},{"key":"B","html":"7"},{"key":"C","html":"9"},{"key":"D","html":"10"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWord closest in meaning to<br />\n\tJEOPARDIZE</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Sagacious"},{"key":"B","html":"Imperil"},{"key":"C","html":"Protect"},{"key":"D","html":"Breakthrough"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • MECHANICAL ENGINEERING • Expert • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of these words is closest in meaning to the word provided?<br />\n\t<strong>CAPTIOUS</strong></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"fault finding"},{"key":"B","html":"insecure"},{"key":"C","html":"changeable"},{"key":"D","html":"vain"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)<br />\n\tCaptious means tending to find fault. Synonym: Fault finding</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tAn unbiased coin is tossed an infinite number of times. The probability that the fourth head appears at the tenth toss is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.048"},{"key":"B","html":"0.082"},{"key":"C","html":"0.657"},{"key":"D","html":"0.092"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/530d591b48dc9ddbdc9b.png\" /></p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The time complexity of Variable Elimination can be exponential in the size of the largest factor created during the elimination process.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Variable Elimination can only handle Bayesian networks with discrete variables.<br />\n\tWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)<br />\n\t&bull; The time complexity indeed can become exponential based on the size of the factors created. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; Variable Elimination can also handle continuous variables, though it might require different approaches. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Number of unlabelled binary trees are same as number of binary search trees for any number of vertices."},{"key":"B","html":"Post-order traversal takes Ο(V) time complexity for V vertices in a binary tree."},{"key":"C","html":"Pre-order and post-order traversal combination can always uniquely construct a binary tree."},{"key":"D","html":"In a binary search tree, the smallest vertex always lie in one of leaf nodes."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c0914faa35b0f0d33021.png\" /></p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider Vamshi decides to toss a fair coin repeatedly until he gets a tail. He makes atmost 4 tosses. The value of variance (T) is ________</p>\n<p>\n\t(variable T denotes the number of tosses).</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/9108e45db9e2d36dae26.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddef6610d33fa77d7b82.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9c/9c82d4b64c43439e76b4.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e757cf8a27eea600114.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/4107d2edde5c62472676.png\" /></p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tLet <em>P</em>(<em>X</em>) = 0.45, <em>P</em>(<em>Y</em>) = 0.65 and <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> ∩ <em>Y</em>) = 0.25. Which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b19dce45aac88c9d98d8.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tThe two event <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are dependent.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e8c756bc7d109bf91b7a.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/24/24ffe7ee92df8d2f7838.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be3024f1c2fd43ffb8f6.png\" /></p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tArray [A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J] represents a binary min-heap containing 10 items, where the key of each item is a distinct integer. Then, which of the following statement(s) is/are correct with respect to the item(s) in the array could have the key with?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Smallest integer can corresponds to A."},{"key":"B","html":"Largest integer can corresponds to any of H, I, J only."},{"key":"C","html":"Third smallest integer can corresponds to any of B, C only."},{"key":"D","html":"Third smallest integer can corresponds to any of B, C, D, E, F, G."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d6/d6f923edc271411b83cc.png\" /></p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Complete linkage clustering tends to produce compact clusters with roughly equal diameters.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The R-squared value in Linear Regression represents variance explained.<br />\n\tWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\tNeither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\t&bull; Complete linkage clustering computes the distance between the farthest points in two clusters, producing compact clusters with uniform sizes and shapes. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; The R-squared value (coefficient of determination) measures the proportion of variance in the dependent variable predictable from the independent variables. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Decision criterion in a decision tree is used to determine the best feature to split the data.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Decision criterion compares feature values against a purity measure make splitting decisions.<br />\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tBoth <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\t&bull; The decision criterion helps choose the best feature for splitting the data at each node of the decision tree. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; the decision criterion often compares feature values to a threshold (such as in Gini impurity, entropy, or other splitting metrics) to determine how to split the data. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following relation R(X, Y, Z, W, V) with given FD’s<br />\n\t{XY → Z, YZ → W, ZW → X, XW → Y}<br />\n\tNumber of super keys in relation <em>R</em> ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[9,9],"answerText":"9","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t9</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3cd629140ab92bf5a499.png\" /></p>"},{"n":20,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following Python code:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10be0a778a7b3b9f9a50.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tThe output of this program will be ______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-12,-12],"answerText":"-12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t-12</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3c0e654d522051180ce4.png\" /></p>"},{"n":21,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWe are interested in topological orderings of the following DAG that satisfy one or both of the following constraints:<br />\n\t1. 4 appears before 3<br />\n\t2. 8 appears after 7<br />\n\tHow many such orderings are there?</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46ccaf02253d08ffbe9a.png\" /></p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[16,16],"answerText":"16","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t16<br />\n\tTopological sort is of the form 1, followed by a topological ordering of {2, 3, 4, 5}, followed by 6,<br />\n\tfollowed by a topological ordering {7, 8, 9}. Nodes {2, 3, 4, 5} have (4 choose 2) = 6 topological orderings of which only one, 2-3-4-5 has 3 before 4. All other orderings have 4 before 3: 2-4-3-5,<br />\n\t2-4-5-3, 4-2-3-5, 4-2-5-3, 4-5-2-3.<br />\n\tNodes {7, 8, 9} have 3 topological orderings, 7-8-9, 8-7-9, 8-9-7, of which two have 8 before 7 and one has 8 after 7. There are 6 &times; 3 = 18 total topological orderings of which 1 &times; 2 = 2 violates both<br />\n\tconditions, so 16 satisfy one condition or both.</p>"},{"n":22,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tIf <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/7c/7cef0585a9db41b87dd1.png\" /> is equal to ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[21,21],"answerText":"21","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t21</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fd/fd32af3dbd74ee494083.png\" /></p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tA factory produces equal amounts of two types of batteries. The probability that a battery of Type A losts for more than 500 charges is 0.85, and for Type B, the probability is 0.65. What is the probability that a randomly chosen battery losts for more than 500 charges?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.85"},{"key":"B","html":"0.65"},{"key":"C","html":"0.75"},{"key":"D","html":"0.95"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f92f98d128f92139c3ee.png\" /></p>"},{"n":24,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following graph G:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33cea8704343e151e3c4.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tWe applied Dijkstra’s algorithm for single source shortest path on graph <em>G</em> and node ‘<em>a</em>’ is source node. Find the number of nodes including node ‘a’ for which algorithm has calculated the shortest path before finding shortest path of node ‘<em>f</em> ’?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6 (6 - 6)","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t6 (6 - 6)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2e95b25272a2a40ffacd.png\" /></p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tMatch <strong>List-I</strong> with <strong>List-II</strong> and select the correct answer using the codes given below the lists:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/24/24d901a9abf17d5378af.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\t&bull; Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) &rarr; Non-Linear Decision Boundaries<br />\n\t&bull; Decision Tree &rarr; Discriminative Model<br />\n\t&bull; Bias-Variance Trade-Off &rarr; Generalization Error<br />\n\t&bull; Cross-Validation &rarr; Model Tuning Technique</p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following graph G:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee023dce2ce7181ee075.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tMinimum spanning tree of G is unique and includes edges shown in figure with weight <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/1615dc287566ceb81c38.png\" /> and y.<br />\n\tWhat is the maximum possible value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/1615dc287566ceb81c38.png\" /> + y?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"11"},{"key":"B","html":"12"},{"key":"C","html":"13"},{"key":"D","html":"14"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8bfb02f67ddfb18edf5c.png\" /></p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The OLAP database is not often updated; as a result, data integrity is unaffected.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In an OLAP database, tables are normalized.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : The data integrity constraint must be maintained in an OLTP database.<br />\n\tWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\t&bull; As OLAP databases are typically not frequently updated, minimizing data. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; OLAP databases are usually denormalized to enhance query performance. Integrity concerns. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; Data integrity constraints are crucial in OLTP databases to ensure accurate transactions. So, <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tA manufacturer of cotter pins knows that 5% of his product is defective. Pins are sold in boxes of 100. He guarantees that not more than 10 pins will be defective. What is the approximate probability that a box will fail to meet the guaranteed quality?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.0140"},{"key":"B","html":"0.0137"},{"key":"C","html":"0.0157"},{"key":"D","html":"0.0130"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d1ebc881d119435667ae.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/65/655bc5c3c419706676c1.png\" /></p>"},{"n":29,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a hash table of length 10 using open addressing with hash function h(k) = k mod 10 and linear probing. Initially, table is empty. Now insert following keys into table in given order:<br />\n\t13, 22, 6, 18, 2, 34, 12, 9, 17<br />\n\tTotal number of collisions occurs or above insertions is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[11,11],"answerText":"11","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t11</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6a0ecd486d2756c41547.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5a36831ed79de5736145.png\" /></p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\t<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fd/fd58af71a9912da7efe7.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<em>e</em></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"Undefined"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/692acdee3d7d59289664.png\" /></p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub>  : Leave-One-Out (LOO) cross-validation uses each data point as a test set once.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The dual problem of SVM involves minimizing the Lagrangian dual function.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : K-means clustering assigns data points to the nearest cluster centroid based on distance.<br />\n\tWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\t&bull; LOO cross-validation, where each individual data point is used as a test set exactly once, while the rest of the data points serve as the training set. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; In SVM, the primal problem aims to minimize a cost function, while the dual problem focuses on maximizing the dual function. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; K-means clustering operates, assigning data points to the nearest cluster centroid. So, <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\t“No teachers are allowed to carry books into the exam hall”.<br />\n\tWhich of the following formula is correct for the above English sentence?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5be897ae98362e9fbf0f.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ec/ec11c831f20bf5ee161e.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bd/bdde461d3c5b1abc64e2.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d083c0d39ddd68568c5.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab925330cc101180210b.png\" /></p>"},{"n":33,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a relation <em>R</em> (<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>, <em>D</em>, <em>E</em>) with the following functional dependencies:<br />\n\t<em>A</em> → <em>BC</em>, <em>C</em> → <em>E</em>, <em>B</em> → <em>D</em>, <em>E</em> → <em>A</em><br />\n\tThe total number of super keys present in the relation are _______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[28,28],"answerText":"28","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t28</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/645d4c5f347e9e4ab0bc.png\" /></p>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/694d49a001a366e8ed7f.png\" /> be a function defined by</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4da974bc3a82f6858183.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tIn <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/95/957812d731c488f16abd.png\" /> exist, then the value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3a32b332565f5e305706.png\" /> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-1,-1],"answerText":"-1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t-1</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cc42c6c1a10937ca8340.png\" /></p>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tFor the two independent events α and β, the probability that both occur is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23e5f6495cd3ed4c30a7.png\" /> and probability that neither of them occur is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a919fd541f1c7fe233e.png\" /> If the probability of occurrence of α and β is <em>P</em>(α) and <em>P</em>(β), then <em>P</em>(α) + <em>P</em>(β) is ________. (Upto two decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.93,0.98],"answerText":"0.95 [0.93 - 0.98]","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t0.95 [0.93 - 0.98]</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f1/f16480eff91ec84b92bf.png\" /></p>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tMatch the following Lists.</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/3664b125f4827203911a.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02018dd925fa5d6d18fc.png\" /></p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following functions and arrange then in increasing order:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/62b424c80f49f8e3a24e.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I, III, II, IV, V"},{"key":"B","html":"I, III, IV, II, V"},{"key":"C","html":"I, II, III, IV, V"},{"key":"D","html":"V, IV, III, II, I"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da04c809c0672289d46d.png\" /></p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tIn <em>k</em>-Nearest Neighbors (<em>k</em>-NN), the classification is based on the majority vote among the k nearest neighbors.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"Lasso Regression includes all features in the final model, regardless of their relevance."},{"key":"C","html":"Principal Component Analysis (PCA) uses Euclidean distance to perform dimensionality reduction."},{"key":"D","html":"Logistic Regression estimates the probability of a binary outcome using a linear function."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)<br />\n\t&bull; k-NN classifies based on the majority vote among its k nearest neighbors. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; Lasso Regression can set some coefficients to zero, effectively excluding less relevant features. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; PCA performs dimensionality reduction using orthogonal transformations, not Euclidean distance. So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; Logistic Regression uses the logistic function (sigmoid) to estimate probabilities, not a linear function. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements regarding the star schema:<br />\n\t(a) The star schema requires more storage space than snowflake schemas due to denormalization.<br />\n\t(b) The star schema has more foreign keys than snowflake schemas because of its normalized structure.<br />\n\t(c) The denormalized structure of the star schema enhances query performance by reducing joins.<br />\n\t(d) Star schemas are easier to understand and design due to their straightforward structure, making them user-friendly for analysts.<br />\n\tWhich of the above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, c, d)<br />\n\t&bull; The star schema requires more storage space than snowflake schemas due to denormalization, which leads to redundancy. So, option (a) is true.<br />\n\t&bull; The star schema has fewer foreign keys compared to snowflake schemas due to its simpler structure. So, option (b) is false.<br />\n\t&bull; The denormalized structure of the star schema enhances query performance by reducing the number of joins needed. So, option (c) is true.<br />\n\t&bull; Star schemas are easier to understand and design because of their straightforward structure, making them user-friendly for analysts. So, option (d) is true.</p>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Python popularly used for mobile applications."},{"key":"B","html":"Python does not support oops concepts."},{"key":"C","html":"Python does not support Numpy arrays"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the 3 × 3 non-singular matrix:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/5027201f338548c5ea33.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tIt is given that on of the eigen value is –2. The eigen vector corresponding to the highest eigen value is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/21/21214b700a4bfc08cbcb.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b7/b7ea6b5aefbd619c9ed9.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8ff0a9d98a5c0141b14c.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a4d983019ea54a46bd0.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f4bdea9505d1c91e6e9a.png\" /></p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statement is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fd/fd14651dd5369ec92b2e.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tThe rank of a matrix is ‘<em>r</em>’ if determinant of every minor of order (<em>r</em> + 1) is zero.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\tIf <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/56/56c346fbcc5bb67429c2.png\" /> is an eigen value of an orthogonal matrix, then <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f5cd5e28347d1c423b0.png\" /> is also an eigen value of its inverse matrix.</p>"},{"key":"D","html":"Maximum number of distinct eigen values of matrix A <size of matrix A."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39acde5a0174b0f749d0.png\" /></p>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t(a) Naive Bayes classifier is effective for handling continuous features.<br />\n\t(b) Ridge regression adds a penalty to the cost function to handle multicollinearity.<br />\n\t(c) PCA helps in transforming data into principal components for feature extraction.<br />\n\t(d) Decision tree used for dimensionality reduction in unsupervised learning.<br />\n\tWhich of the above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b, c)<br />\n\t&bull; Naive Bayes is primarily effective with categorical features. For continuous features, it often assumes that they follow a Gaussian distribution, which may not always be valid. So, option<br />\n\t(a) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; Ridge regression introduces an L2 penalty (regularization term) to the cost function, which helps to mitigate multicollinearity among predictors by shrinking the coefficients. So, option<br />\n\t(b) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is used for feature extraction by transforming the original features into a new set of orthogonal features (principal components), which capture the maximum variance in the data. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; Decision trees are primarily used for supervised learning tasks like classification and regression. While they can help in feature selection, they are not typically used for dimensionality reduction in an unsupervised manner. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":44,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tGiven a dataset that contains three classes : Class A, Class B, and Class C—where Class A has 5 examples, Class B has 3 examples, and Class C has 2 examples, calculate the Gini index for this dataset.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.62,0.62],"answerText":"0.62","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t0.62</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28d803247005fdcf3da7.png\" /></p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following elements:<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Swap elements <em>A</em>[<em>n</em>] to A[1].<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Now root may violate max heap property, but its children are max-heaps. Run max-heapify to fix this.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Continue until heap is empty.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : Build max-heap from unordered array.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>5</sub> : Discard node <em>n</em> from heap by decrementing heap-size variable.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>6</sub> : Find the maximum element <em>A</em>[1].<br />\n\t[<strong>Note:</strong> Array indices are in the range of 1 to n]<br />\n\tSuppose a max-heap is implemented using array as the data structure. Find the correct sequence of above statements for heap sort algorithm using max heap:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/52/524e66f66bf5325f9096.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d0e181dcfd27e4afe1e.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/43e65944e2fd519bdba4.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb565c3c7481783f8887.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tNiraj try repeatedly to get a seat reservation in any train running between Delhi to Patna until he successful. If there is 20% chance of getting reservation in any attempt by a passenger then the average number of attempts that Niraj need to make to get a seat reserved is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t5</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/018d6368ec9804ba390e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following professor table:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/01f96fc1be96fd5c9f5d.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tHow many row will be returned by he following query?</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/099f076d9a890f2654c3.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tThree white and three black balls, kept in three bins are arranged in four ways as shown below.<br />\n\tIn each arrangement a bin has to be chosen randomly and only one ball needs to be picked randomly from the chosen bin.<br />\n\tWhich of the following arrangements has the probability for getting a write ball picked less than or equal to 0.5?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d59a147f3d1df97d878.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa90d74855752580f9d5.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/97/971aea8d238505e24949.png\" /></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73f39344dc71d8a0a809.png\" /></p>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b, c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39b228b97f489ce79616.png\" /></p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tThe value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6ad4b12c530c22e6e7d.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\te<sup>10</sup></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\te<sup>6</sup></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"<p>\n\te<sup>5</sup></p>"},{"key":"D","html":"<p>\n\te<sup>2</sup></p>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/8583be947aef071941a9.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ed/ed4d821fbbf78c20fa18.png\" /></p>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements about the Support Vector Machine (SVM) is/are correct?<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The decision function for an SVM is given by <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa30463e76153bd6e81d.png\" /><br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In the context of a soft-margin SVM, the regularization parameter C controls the trade-off between maximizing the margin and minimizing the classification error.<br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : The margin of an SVM is defined as <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28d1b6b0f2c49d1c7718.png\" /><br />\n\t<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : The Kernel function in an SVM allows the transformation of the input data into a higherdimensional space to make it linearly separable.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>1</sub></p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tOnly <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></p>"},{"key":"C","html":"All of the above"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(c)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5da80eace3a998af2b64.png\" /></p>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tIf 3 is an eigen value of a diagonal matrix [<em>A</em>]<sub>3 × 3</sub> with same diagonal elements then the eigen values of A + adj(A)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3, 3, 3"},{"key":"B","html":"9, 9, 9"},{"key":"C","html":"27, 27, 27"},{"key":"D","html":"12, 12, 12"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64efc6f556c731d4a629.png\" /></p>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4cdfc903d0f8f526b45a.png\" /> The minimum value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1feeaac1dab138765759.png\" /> over the interval [0, 2] is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t6</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6c0a18f767f6f7f6946f.png\" /></p>"},{"n":53,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider an SVM with a polynomial Kernel of degree 2:</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0af7b65d637588e6b512.png\" /></p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[36,36],"answerText":"36","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t36</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ffdd19a1fd3d87575de8.png\" /></p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tLet <em>R</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>) is a relation schema. Using BCNF decomposition algorithm, <em>R</em> decomposed into <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>(A, B) and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>A</em>, <em>C</em>) based on <em>A</em> → <em>B</em> functional dependency which holds on <em>R</em>. Which of the following is/are correct for above decomposition?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<p>\n\tA is candidate key of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>, always.</p>"},{"key":"B","html":"<p>\n\tA is candidate key of <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>, always.</p>"},{"key":"C","html":"Above decomposition may not be in BCNF in some situation."},{"key":"D","html":"Above decomposition is always in 3NF."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/08/086dd0c4e206496aadf6.png\" /></p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t(a) The Mini-Max algorithm uses recursion to explore the game-tree for optimal moves.<br />\n\t(b) The Mini-Max algorithm assumes both players play optimally, alternating between maximizing and minimizing scores.<br />\n\t(c) A* maintains a priority order based on <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>).<br />\n\t(d) A* stores only a limited number of nodes in the priority queue, making it memory-efficient.<br />\n\tWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b)<br />\n\t&bull; The Mini-Max algorithm indeed uses recursion to traverse the game-tree and evaluate the possible moves to find the optimal one. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; The Mini-Max algorithm operates under the assumption that both players are playing<br />\n\toptimally, maximizing and minimizing their scores alternately. So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n\t&bull; A* maintains a priority order based on both the cost g(n) (cost to reach node n) and the heuristic h(n) (estimated cost from n to the goal), usually expressed as <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) = <em>g</em>(<em>n</em>) + h(n).<br />\n\tSo, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; A* can store many nodes in its priority queue depending on the state space. It is not necessarily memory-efficient, especially in large search spaces. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a4/a4ca620436c6cca35472.png\" /> be a function defined by</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b853fa82c99677c5ef8.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\tIn <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a050d04d204698fe12d.png\" /> exist, then the value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b5da3ccbee0562203d9a.png\" /> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-1,-1],"answerText":"-1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t-1</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/8805eb6c425d0023203f.png\" /></p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tConsider the following statements:<br />\n\t(a) Prim’s algorithm can be implemented using Fibonacci heap.<br />\n\t(b) Prim’s algorithm construct MST by selection edge in increasing order of their weights.<br />\n\t(c) BFS take Ο(<em>V</em><sup>2</sup>) time in a graph <em>G</em>(<em>V</em>, <em>E</em>) if graph is represented with an adjacency matrix.<br />\n\t(d) For a directed graph, the absence of back edges in a DFS tree means graph has cycle.<br />\n\tWhich of the above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, c)<br />\n\t&bull; Prim&rsquo;s algorithm grows the MST by adding the smallest edge from the current tree to a new vertex not by globally selection edges in increasing order of weights. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n\t&bull; For a directed graph, the absence of back edges in a DFS tree means graph has not cycle. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tIf the rank of matrix <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03d28a428a8b707df96a.png\" /> is 3, then the possible values (<em>s</em>) of <em>b</em> is/are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"-6"},{"key":"C","html":"3"},{"key":"D","html":"8"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, b)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b8fab779e864304e0941.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/26b93151d39ce62606db.png\" /></p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\t<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7beb50ca8865ff42a342.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"α > β"},{"key":"B","html":"α < β"},{"key":"C","html":"α = β"},{"key":"D","html":"β = 0"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a, d)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e04da6398ef198b050e.png\" /></p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tWhich of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Deletion of the first element in a circular doubly linked list is Ο(n). Where n is the number of elements in the linked list."},{"key":"B","html":"Deletion of the first element in a circular singly linked list is Ο(n)."},{"key":"C","html":"Searching time complexity of binary search is Ο(n) when linked list are used."},{"key":"D","html":"Insertion of a node in between a doubly linked list requires 4 linked list pointer updates."}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b, c, d)</p>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tConsider a file of 8192 records. Each record is 16 bytes long and its key field is of size 6 bytes. The file is ordered on a key field, and the file organization is unspanned. The file is stored in a file system with block size 512 bytes, and the size of a block pointer is 10 bytes. If the primary index is built on the key field of the file, and a multilevel index scheme is used to store the primary index, the number of first-level and second-level blocks in the multilevel index are respectively</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8 and 1"},{"key":"B","html":"16 and 1"},{"key":"C","html":"8 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d6/d6db61be001be1fe52ac.png\" /></p>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tIn a K-class dataset, you decide to use a Naive Bayes classifier. The dataset contains N points, each described using D features, where each feature is categorical and can take V values. If you merge two of the K classes into a single class, how does this affect the number of parameters to estimate?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The number of parameters increases."},{"key":"B","html":"The number of parameters decreases."},{"key":"C","html":"The number of parameters remains the same."},{"key":"D","html":"The number of parameters depends on D."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(b)<br />\n\tIf you merge two classes, the total number of classes K decreases by 1, which reduces the number of parameters that need to be estimated. Specifically, for the Naive Bayes classifier.<br />\n\t&bull; Class Priors: The number of class priors decreases from K to K &ndash; 1.<br />\n\t&bull; Conditional Probabilities: The number of conditional probabilities decreases from K &times; D &times; V to (K &ndash; 1) &times; D &times; V.<br />\n\tThe correct answer is option (b).</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>\n\tThe number of binary search trees possible with n nodes such that each nodes takes a value from the set {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} root has value 3 and the left child of the root has value 2, is equal to ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5"},{"key":"B","html":"6"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"7"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/4886840fd9b43d80463d.png\" /></p>\n<p>\n\t&nbsp;</p>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>\n\tA continuous random variable x has the probability density function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3ca459899bf7fdf2fb2.png\" /> and <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/798cea304d6ebaea5041.png\" /> else where</p>\n<p>\n\tIf the mean of the distribution is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa9f14ddca84e26e2792.png\" /> then the value of α + β is________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>\n\t(a)</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b80da322eb068bebce00.png\" /></p>"},{"n":65,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science and artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>\n\tA dataset contains two classes: Positive and Negative. The dataset has the following distribution of examples:<br />\n\tPositive class: 6 examples<br />\n\tNegative class: 4 examples<br />\n\tCalculate the entropy of this dataset. (Upto to 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.92,0.99],"answerText":"0.97 [0.92 - 0.99]","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>\n\t0.97 [0.92 - 0.99]</p>\n<p>\n\t<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/70db1aba3386535fe0cf.png\" /></p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":34,"NAT":16,"MSQ":15},"withoutKey":0}};
